# State Management Guidelines

## Overview

This project uses **Unidirectional Data Flow (UDF)** pattern with ViewModels and Compose State.

## State Management Rules

### 1. State Lives in ViewModel
```kotlin
@HiltViewModel
class MyViewModel @Inject constructor(
    private val useCase: MyUseCase
) : ViewModel() {
    
    // Mutable state (private)
    private val _uiState = MutableStateFlow(MyUiState())
    
    // Immutable state (public)
    val uiState: StateFlow<MyUiState> = _uiState.asStateFlow()
    
    // Events/Actions
    fun onUserAction() {
        _uiState.update { it.copy(/* new state */) }
    }
}
```

### 2. UI State as Data Class
```kotlin
data class MyUiState(
    val isLoading: Boolean = false,
    val data: List<Item> = emptyList(),
    val error: String? = null,
    val isSuccess: Boolean = false
)
```

### 3. Use StateFlow for State
✅ **GOOD**:
```kotlin
private val _uiState = MutableStateFlow(MyUiState())
val uiState: StateFlow<MyUiState> = _uiState.asStateFlow()
```

❌ **BAD** (Don't use LiveData in new code):
```kotlin
private val _uiState = MutableLiveData<MyUiState>()
val uiState: LiveData<MyUiState> = _uiState
```

## UI State Patterns

### Pattern 1: Loading, Content, Error
```kotlin
data class ScreenUiState(
    val isLoading: Boolean = false,
    val data: List<Item>? = null,
    val error: String? = null
)

// In Composable
when {
    uiState.isLoading -> LoadingIndicator()
    uiState.error != null -> ErrorView(uiState.error)
    uiState.data != null -> ContentView(uiState.data)
}
```

### Pattern 2: Sealed Class State
```kotlin
sealed interface ScreenState {
    object Loading : ScreenState
    data class Success(val data: List<Item>) : ScreenState
    data class Error(val message: String) : ScreenState
}

// In Composable
when (val state = uiState.value) {
    is ScreenState.Loading -> LoadingIndicator()
    is ScreenState.Success -> ContentView(state.data)
    is ScreenState.Error -> ErrorView(state.message)
}
```

### Pattern 3: Multiple State Properties
```kotlin
data class FormUiState(
    val email: String = "",
    val emailError: String? = null,
    val password: String = "",
    val passwordError: String? = null,
    val isLoading: Boolean = false,
    val isSubmitEnabled: Boolean = false
)
```

## State Updates

### 1. Use `update` for StateFlow
```kotlin
fun onEmailChange(email: String) {
    _uiState.update { currentState ->
        currentState.copy(
            email = email,
            emailError = validateEmail(email)
        )
    }
}
```

### 2. Don't Mutate State Directly
❌ **BAD**:
```kotlin
_uiState.value.email = "new@email.com"  // DON'T
```

✅ **GOOD**:
```kotlin
_uiState.update { it.copy(email = "new@email.com") }
```

## Collecting State in Composables

### Use `collectAsStateWithLifecycle()`
```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyScreen(viewModel: MyViewModel = hiltViewModel()) {
    val uiState by viewModel.uiState.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()
    
    // Use uiState
    MyContent(uiState = uiState)
}
```

### For Multiple Flows
```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyScreen(viewModel: MyViewModel = hiltViewModel()) {
    val uiState by viewModel.uiState.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()
    val events by viewModel.events.collectAsStateWithLifecycle(initialValue = null)
    
    // Handle events
    LaunchedEffect(events) {
        events?.let { event ->
            // Handle one-time event
        }
    }
}
```

## Side Effects

### 1. Use LaunchedEffect for One-Time Events
```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyScreen(viewModel: MyViewModel = hiltViewModel()) {
    val uiState by viewModel.uiState.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()
    val navigator = LocalNavigator.currentOrThrow
    
    LaunchedEffect(uiState.isSuccess) {
        if (uiState.isSuccess) {
            navigator.push(NextScreen())
        }
    }
}
```

### 2. Use DisposableEffect for Cleanup
```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyScreen(viewModel: MyViewModel = hiltViewModel()) {
    DisposableEffect(Unit) {
        viewModel.startListening()
        onDispose {
            viewModel.stopListening()
        }
    }
}
```

### 3. Use derivedStateOf for Computed State
```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyScreen(items: List<Item>) {
    val itemCount by remember { 
        derivedStateOf { items.size }
    }
    
    Text("Total: $itemCount")
}
```

## Event Handling

### Pattern 1: Single Event Flow
```kotlin
@HiltViewModel
class MyViewModel @Inject constructor() : ViewModel() {
    
    private val _events = Channel<MyEvent>()
    val events = _events.receiveAsFlow()
    
    fun triggerEvent() {
        viewModelScope.launch {
            _events.send(MyEvent.ShowSnackbar("Success"))
        }
    }
}

// In Composable
LaunchedEffect(Unit) {
    viewModel.events.collect { event ->
        when (event) {
            is MyEvent.ShowSnackbar -> {
                snackbarHostState.showSnackbar(event.message)
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Pattern 2: Event as State Property
```kotlin
data class UiState(
    val data: List<Item> = emptyList(),
    val showSuccessMessage: Boolean = false
)

// Clear after handling
LaunchedEffect(uiState.showSuccessMessage) {
    if (uiState.showSuccessMessage) {
        snackbarHostState.showSnackbar("Success")
        viewModel.clearSuccessMessage()
    }
}
```

## Async Operations in ViewModel

### Use viewModelScope
```kotlin
@HiltViewModel
class MyViewModel @Inject constructor(
    private val useCase: MyUseCase
) : ViewModel() {
    
    fun loadData() {
        viewModelScope.launch {
            _uiState.update { it.copy(isLoading = true) }
            
            useCase()
                .catch { exception ->
                    _uiState.update { 
                        it.copy(
                            isLoading = false,
                            error = exception.message
                        )
                    }
                }
                .collect { result ->
                    _uiState.update {
                        it.copy(
                            isLoading = false,
                            data = result,
                            error = null
                        )
                    }
                }
        }
    }
}
```

## State Hoisting

### Hoist State When Reusing Components
```kotlin
// Stateless component
@Composable
fun TextField(
    value: String,
    onValueChange: (String) -> Unit,
    modifier: Modifier = Modifier
) {
    // Component is stateless
}

// State in parent
@Composable
fun MyScreen() {
    var text by remember { mutableStateOf("") }
    
    TextField(
        value = text,
        onValueChange = { text = it }
    )
}
```

## Testing State

### Unit Test State Changes
```kotlin
@Test
fun `loadData should update state to loading then success`() = runTest {
    // Given
    val testData = listOf(Item(1), Item(2))
    coEvery { useCase() } returns flowOf(testData)
    
    // When
    viewModel.loadData()
    
    // Then
    viewModel.uiState.test {
        val loadingState = awaitItem()
        assertTrue(loadingState.isLoading)
        
        val successState = awaitItem()
        assertFalse(successState.isLoading)
        assertEquals(testData, successState.data)
    }
}
```

## Checklist

- [ ] State is defined in ViewModel
- [ ] State is immutable (exposed as StateFlow/State)
- [ ] State updates use `copy()` or `update()`
- [ ] UI collects state with `collectAsStateWithLifecycle()`
- [ ] Side effects use appropriate Effect APIs
- [ ] Async operations use `viewModelScope`
- [ ] State is testable
- [ ] No business logic in Composables

## Common Mistakes

❌ **DON'T**:
- Mutate state directly
- Use LiveData in new code
- Put business logic in Composables
- Forget lifecycle awareness
- Create state in Composables for screen-level state

✅ **DO**:
- Use StateFlow for state
- Keep state immutable
- Use `update()` for state changes
- Collect with `collectAsStateWithLifecycle()`
- Test state changes
- Hoist state when appropriate